{{ goal_section }}You are building the destination knowledge base that powers a retrieval-based travel assistant. Below is raw deep-research text (it may contain several concatenated research reports, plus repetition, contradictions, and marketing fluff). Once built, travelers will ask a vast and unpredictable range of questions about this destination, so the knowledge base must be as COMPREHENSIVE as possible — not a summary.

Raw research:

{{ retrieved_evidence }}

Transform the research into a set of long-form knowledge segments, one per topic. Rules, in priority order:

1. COVERAGE FIRST. Produce 8–15 segments that together span the full breadth of traveler questions. Cover at least: history, culture, neighborhoods, where_to_stay, attractions_landmarks, itineraries, day_trips, food_drink, nightlife, shopping, transport, airport_transfers, safety_scams, customs_etiquette, language_basics, money_tipping, connectivity, weather_seasons, events_festivals, accessibility, family_travel. Add extra segments whenever the research contains rich material on other themes.
2. PRESERVE EVERY CONCRETE FACT from the research: place names, neighborhoods, dish names, transit lines and stops, ticket/pass names, typical costs, opening patterns, seasonal timing, explicit warnings. Never compress a list of ten specific places into "many options".
3. ENRICH, DO NOT SUMMARIZE. Expand each segment with your own reliable, well-established knowledge about this destination. The research is the backbone; your knowledge fills gaps and adds depth. Each finished segment should read like a thorough guidebook chapter, combining the research with what you know.
4. For volatile specifics (exact prices, hours, schedules) that are not confirmed by the research, give typical ranges and phrase them as approximate rather than inventing precise figures.
5. Merge duplicated or overlapping material across the reports. When sources contradict each other, prefer the research over your priors.
6. Each segment must be self-contained prose of roughly 250–600 words — several well-developed paragraphs, understandable with no other context. Short or fragmentary sections are a failure.
7. Use short lowercase topic labels (snake_case where needed, e.g. "safety_scams", "day_trips").

